"""Check the wasm32 clippy guard: its configuration, the settings that could
switch it off, and that it refuses a call to each entry.

The guard is the clippy step of the WebAssembly workflow, run with
CLIPPY_CONF_DIR pointing at devtools/wasm-clippy, whose clippy.toml lists
methods and types. A path there that does not resolve only makes clippy print
a warning, and under -D warnings the step still exits 0 (clippy 0.1.97), so a
misspelt entry would leave the step green with that call unguarded. The step
also runs clippy's await_holding_lock and await_holding_refcell_ref lints,
which warn by default and report a std Mutex or RwLock guard, or a RefCell
borrow, held across an .await; on wasm32-unknown-unknown such code traps when
another task takes a conflicting lock or borrow of the same value (std's Mutex
panics with "cannot recursively acquire mutex"). A setting that switches one
of the two disallowed lints or one of those two lints off, or keeps its
reports warnings, directly or through clippy::all, clippy::style,
clippy::suspicious or warnings, switches the guard off for the code it
covers, and the step again exits 0: for example an allow of one of them,
#![warn(warnings)] (the step's -D warnings then leaves every warning of that
code a warning), or a --force-warn or --cap-lints in rustflags. This script
fails, with one line per problem, unless all of these hold:

1. clippy.toml holds only `disallowed-methods` and `disallowed-types`; each
   entry is a table with a `path` and a `reason` and nothing else; no path is
   listed twice; each entry has a probe below and each probe names an entry.
2. In the crates the wasm build compiles (extenddb-wasm-adapter and the
   workspace crates it depends on for wasm32, read from `cargo metadata`), the
   script runs the clippy step in the working tree and reads every file rustc
   lists in each crate's dep-info: its source files wherever they are (a
   module that #[path] names included), and every file that include!,
   include_str! or include_bytes! reads, each read as Rust source; the
   Cargo.toml and the clippy configuration that clippy adds there are left
   out. A crate the step leaves no dep-info for, or a step that fails, is a
   problem. In those files no attribute allows, expects or warns clippy::all,
   clippy::style, clippy::suspicious or warnings, nor
   clippy::await_holding_lock or clippy::await_holding_refcell_ref with or
   without a reason; none warns clippy::disallowed_methods or
   clippy::disallowed_types (or their former names), and none allows or
   expects one of those two without a `reason` that holds more than
   whitespace. An allow or expect of one of those two, with a reason, is
   refused as an inner attribute (`#![...]`), which covers all of its
   module, crate or function, and on a module, an impl block or a trait,
   which covers every function in it; clippy.toml asks for it on the
   function or the statement that needs it. A lint named with a raw
   identifier (`clippy::r#all`) is read as rustc reads it, as the plain name.
   The one exception is a catalog migration's code-body module:
   CONTRIBUTING.md ("Adding a catalog migration") declares each one, in
   src/migrations/mod.rs, with #[rustfmt::skip] and #[allow(clippy::all,
   deprecated)], because its body is checksummed once released and no lint
   may ask for an edit. An allow of clippy::all is accepted only in that
   exact form, on a `mod mNNNN_<name>;` line of a src/migrations/mod.rs whose
   src/migrations/mNNNN_<name>.rs exists; the guard does not see those
   bodies, and the headless parity corpus runs every migration when it
   creates an engine. The `[lints]`
   tables of those crates and the workspace's `[workspace.lints]` set none of
   these lints or groups to a level other than deny or forbid (a key spelt
   with hyphens is read with underscores, as rustc reads it). No `rustflags`
   setting, in any table of .cargo/config.toml or .cargo/config, holds -A,
   -W, -Z, --allow, --warn, --force-warn or --cap-lints, with its value
   apart, attached or after `=`, or an @-file of further arguments; and
   neither file's [build] table sets rustc, rustc-wrapper or
   rustc-workspace-wrapper, which run another program as the compiler.
   .github/workflows/wasm.yml, read as text, names outside its comments no
   variable that sets rustc flags (RUSTFLAGS, CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS,
   CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS or CARGO_TARGET_<TRIPLE>_RUSTFLAGS) or the compiler
   (RUSTC, RUSTC_WRAPPER, RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER, or CARGO_BUILD_RUSTC and
   its two wrapper forms). A warn of one of these lints or groups, or a -W in
   rustflags, does not stop the step from failing on a guarded call, as its
   -D warnings raises the warning to an error again; they are refused too.
   CLIPPY_ARGS in the workflow is not looked for: cargo clippy sets that
   variable from the arguments after `--` and replaces a value it inherits.
   In the clippy step's run, each crate's dep-info records that
   clippy-driver read CLIPPY_ARGS with -D warnings and none of the flags
   above, and CLIPPY_CONF_DIR naming devtools/wasm-clippy (a build script's
   cargo::rustc-env, or an [env] entry with force = true in the cargo
   configuration, can change either for a crate); and the run reports no
   disallowed-method, disallowed-type or await-holding lint at a level other
   than error, which would let the step pass.
   SETTING_PROBES lists lint settings in each of these spellings (attribute,
   inner attribute, cfg_attr, `[lints]` table, rustflags, workflow lines); the
   functions that read the crates' files and the workflow must refuse or
   accept each one as the list says. FILE_PROBES adds to FILE_PROBE_CRATE, in
   a copy of the working tree, a module outside src/ that #[path] names and a
   file of another extension that include! reads, each with a lint setting
   the scan must refuse; the scan, run on that copy, must read and refuse
   both. DRIVER_PROBES lists values of CLIPPY_ARGS and CLIPPY_CONF_DIR, as a
   crate's dep-info records what clippy-driver read, and reports of the
   clippy step, that the function reading them must refuse or accept as the
   list says.
   Those functions read lint settings as they are written in the files and
   expand no macro: a setting that a macro builds from its input (for example
   `#[$level(clippy::all)]` in a macro_rules! body), or that a procedural
   macro emits, is not seen.
3. In a copy of the working tree (the files git tracks or does not ignore,
   uncommitted edits included), with every probe added to a source file of
   PROBE_CRATE, the clippy step of the workflow fails with clippy's
   disallowed-method or disallowed-type error naming the probed path inside
   each probe, and prints no warning about clippy.toml. MESSAGE_PROBES lists
   compiler messages that the function counting those refusals must count,
   or not count, as refusing a probe, as the list says; a report at the
   warning level must not count.
4. ZEROIZE_CRATES lists the crates of the wasm32 AES-256-GCM stack whose
   `zeroize` feature must be on in the adapter's wasm32 build. In the copy,
   with each of them in turn without that feature in crates/core/Cargo.toml,
   the check of those features must report that crate and no other.

The clippy step runs in the working tree and in the copy with the caller's
clippy.toml and the caller's cargo target directory, so cargo reuses what the
clippy step already checked. Nothing in the caller's working tree but that
target directory is written. The copy is removed on exit, also on SIGINT,
SIGTERM and SIGHUP, and a clippy run is stopped with it. Some probes reach
the method through an import or a re-export (`use tokio::time; time::sleep`,
`tokio::spawn`), as code usually does.

Needs Python 3.11 or later (for tomllib) and git. Run from anywhere in the
repository; exit status 0 means every check passed.
"""

